  "mathematical_comparison": {
    "required_statement": "A basis of an invariant subspace U can be extended to a basis of the finite-dimensional ambient space V over the same field.",
    "correspondence": "exact_for_the_selected_basis_extension_use_with_explicit_field_conditions_and_source_notes",
    "full_selected_proof_read": true,
    "used_prerequisite_arguments_read": true,
    "argument_check": [
      "The exchange proof uses only expansion of a finite linear relation and division by the selected nonzero coefficient. Its second half proves equality of spans.",
      "The size proof exchanges independent vectors into a finite basis. For the dimension bound, spanning by D is unused: if D had more than n elements, choose n+1 distinct elements and run the displayed finite exchange argument.",
      "An added vector outside the current span preserves independence. The existing minus-sign note is necessary in the reverse implication of that lemma; the conclusion remains span membership.",
      "Once the independent set reaches the finite dimension it must span, since otherwise another independent vector would violate the size bound. Therefore extension terminates after finitely many additions.",
      "For dimension zero the only independent set is empty, which is already a basis. This explicit edge-case clarification does not require the positive-n exchange induction.",
      "For the downstream subspace, the same finite size bound permits successively choosing independent vectors within U until it is spanned, so the assumption that U has a finite basis is justified. Extending it to V yields the projection used in Theorem 2.3 and Exercise 5."
    ],
    "improvement_classification": "source-grounded scope and edge-case clarification; no new theorem or novelty claim",
    "no_human_review_claimed": true,
    "not_a_machine_proof": true
  },
